📂 Linux Filesystem Hierarchy — Key Roles & Directories
🌀 Root Directory (/)

This is the top-level directory — the base of all files and directories in Linux. Everything starts from here.

📁 Important Directories Under /
| Directory               | Purpose                                                                                                            |
| ----------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ |
| **`/bin`**              | Essential executable programs (basic commands like `ls`, `cp`, `mv`) needed by all users.  |
| **`/sbin`**             | System **administration binaries** used by the admin/root (like `fsck`, `shutdown`). noraml user cannot access      |
| **`/etc`** (editable text configuration)             | System-wide **configuration files** (network configs, service settings).                  |
| **`/home`**             | Personal home directories for regular users (e.g., `/home/alice`).    each user have directory                     |
| **`/root`**             | Home directory for the **root (admin) user**.                                                   |
| **`/var`**              | **Variable data** — logs, caches, mail, and files that grow/change constantly.                    |
| **`/usr`**              | User programs and utilities (non-essential system apps).                                           |
| **`/tmp`**              | Temporary files (cleared on reboot).                                                      |
| **`/boot`**             | Contains the **kernel and bootloader files** to start the system.                         |
| **`/dev`**              | **Device files** representing hardware devices (disks, terminals).                        |
| **`/mnt` / `/media`**   | Mount points for removable or temporarily attached filesystems.                            |
| **`/proc` / `/sys`**    | **Virtual filesystems** showing process and system info.                                   |
