 /sys                       directory is a virtual filesystem called sysfs. It provides information about hardware devices and kernel subsystems directly from the Linux kernel.

Difference Between /sys and /proc
| `/sys`                                 | `/proc`                            |
| -------------------------------------- | ---------------------------------- |
| Focused on hardware & devices          | Focused on processes & system info |
| Structured device model                | Process-based virtual filesystem   |
| Can sometimes modify kernel parameters | Mostly read-only system info       |


🧠 What “Role” Means in Linux

In Linux, each major directory serves a specific role in how the system works — it keeps things organized so that:

Programs have a predictable place to live

Configurations are kept separate from logs and user files

User data doesn’t get mixed with system files

Administers can control and secure system parts more easily

For example:

/etc holds system settings, not programs.

/bin holds executable commands needed even during system boot.

/var stores files that change over time, like logs.

🧩 How Permissions Fit In (Quick Context)

Linux uses a permission model where files/directories have read/write/execute rights for:

Owner

Group

Others

This determines who can read, write, or execute files — very important for system directories like /etc or /bin.

🧭 Summary

Linux directories are organized in a hierarchical tree (root at top), and each one has a role:

System executables: /bin, /sbin

Configs: /etc

User data: /home

Logs & variable data: /var

Boot files: /boot

Devices: /dev

Temporary data: /tmp

Libraries: /lib, /usr/lib

Application space: /usr, /opt